{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining right-skewness as a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this case, we must assess the skewness of both lotteries. Lottery A has a 1% chance of 0 Euros (unfavorable), 33% chance of 2500 Euros (favorable), and 66% chance of 2400 Euros (also favorable). The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) has only 1% probability, while favorable outcomes (2400 and 2500) together have 99%. Thus, Lottery A is left-skewed (most probability on favorable outcomes). Lottery B has 100% chance of 2400 Euros — a single, certain favorable outcome, with no unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed; in fact, it is perfectly symmetric (no skew). Thus, neither lottery is right-skewed, and the reason would prefer a lottery that is more right-skewed — which does not apply here. Since both lotteries are not right-skewed, the reason cannot be applied to this decision problem. However, the reason is defined in terms of right-skewness, so if the individual evaluates skewness in this way, it would be relevant only if a lottery had a larger share of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Since no such case exists, the reason is not triggered. Therefore, the decision reason cannot be applied to this problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report is: 'A gewählt, weil 0 € extrem unwahrscheinlich.' This translates to: 'I chose A because 0 euros is extremely unlikely.' The individual focuses on the low probability (1%) of receiving 0 Euros in Lottery A, which is perceived as a rare, negative outcome. The individual does not mention probabilities of favorable outcomes, nor does the individual discuss skewness, distribution, or any formal measure of risk or asymmetry. The justification is based on a subjective perception of the rarity of the worst outcome, not on a comparison of skewness. The individual does not consider the distribution of outcomes or the relative probabilities of favorable versus unfavorable outcomes in a way that would align with the definition of right-skewness. The report reflects a concern about the risk of losing money, not a formal evaluation of skewness as defined in the decision reason.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness of the lotteries, specifically identifying which one has a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., right-skewed). In this case, both lotteries have negligible unfavorable probability: Lottery A has 1% chance of 0 Euros, and Lottery B has 0% chance of any unfavorable outcome. Thus, neither is right-skewed, and the reason does not apply. The individual’s justification centers on the perceived rarity of the 0 Euro outcome, which is a subjective risk perception, not a formal assessment of skewness. There is no mention of comparing the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason — which requires evaluating skewness in terms of probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. The reported reasoning is inconsistent with the formal definition of the reason. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
